   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   because ssTEA is meant to work at the mesa level, bits are addressed as mesas define them, but
   the debate over byte/word size was settled back in 1980 (Intel 8086) and 2000 (ARM) dominance,
   which is a pompous way of saying the least significant bit (LSB) is called bit 0 and the LSB
   is the one at the far right, when all the bits are drawn as zeros and ones. just like integers
   are represented with integer digits, and the least significant digit (LSD) is at the far right.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   the idea of byte order and bit order

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The mesa assumption about serial device data is that a human is typing the input and reading the
   output.  There is a variety of buffering and live editing by terminal programs before sending the
   bytes that arrive at the mesa, between human speed and back-to-back serial transmission speed.
   Another assumption is that when the human is typing, the App should pay close attention to input.
   The output, or bytes emitted using the serial port signal carrier, is probably back-to-back at the
   serial transmit baud rate; the HostOS chooses how to drive data through the serial port based on
   the needs of the HostOS.  For some Arduino mesas, a busy port brings the processor to logical halt.
   The mesa provides a buffered transmit service as a solution: a byte is stored if there is room
   and a time-based FSM will send it and any others in "loop". If there is no room, revert to the
   "logical halt" model: emit the oldest byte in the Fifo and THEN put the new byte into the Fifo.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifdef HOST_ONEOF_hostOS_Is_Arduino
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Variables in algorithms are static (existing at all times), or, dynamic (existing for a moment).
   Because FSMs exist over Time, and because FSMs reference values when granted Agency to operate,
   values in the variables for those algorithms must exist over Time.  A dynamic variable is given a
   value at runtime; when the software owning the variable ceases to exist, the value also vanishes.

   The initial value of a static variable may be assigned at build time or run time in several ways.
   The methods are variations on actions taken by the compiler in reaction to ssHL-language found
   describing the variable.  This is a meta-language about the variable beyond the basic language.
   This topic is very advanced for SimpleSal users, so the meta-language is left as an exercise.

   1) a variable referenced but never written may be placed in Read Only memory with the software;
   2) a variable referenced and modified may have a HostOS-defined initial value in Writable memory.
      a) the HostOS can initialize all of memory to a value
      b) the HostOS can copy an image of the build-time value into Writable memory from the image
      c) the HostOS can leave the memory from mesa initialization and use SimpleSal Run Time Init.
   3) a variable never referenced or modified might have the software appearing to do so left out
   4) some co-existing software may require or prohibit certain methods; run time is deterministic.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   // #define MESA_ONEOF_CONTROL_INIT_BUILDTIME
   #define MESA_ONEOF_CONTROL_INIT_RUNTIME

